From: mpc-pi
Subject: [PATCH] mpc2000xl: the panel injection queue was too small, and dropped in silence

0047 made a key event atomic so it could not be split. It could still be thrown
away whole, and it was.

The counter 0047 shipped answered this in one line:

    MPC fast-pad input queue overflow; dropping byte 39 (128 dropped)

Byte 0x39 is the '9' of "PAD CPU V1.00 Feb.15,1999" - the power-on banner. The
queue is 64 bytes and cannot hold the boot handshake and that 26-byte banner
together, so its tail was dropped on every start.

That is the visible half. panel_hle_emit() drops a whole MESSAGE when the queue
is full and does it SILENTLY, so a key pressed during any burst simply never
happened. That is why RESTART still misbehaved after the framing fix: the event
was not being corrupted any more, it was being discarded - which looks exactly
like a controller that never sent it, and is where this search wasted its time.

So: 512 bytes, with 16-bit indices because the old ones were uint8_t and would
wrap, and a warning on the message-drop path so it can never be silent again.
The queue drains a byte every 4us, so 512 is 2ms of backlog at worst and costs
half a kilobyte.

diff --git a/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp b/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp
--- a/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp
+++ b/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp
@@ -239,8 +239,22 @@
 	emu_timer *m_fast_pad_timer;
 	bool m_fast_pad_enabled;
 	uint8_t m_midi_status, m_midi_data_pos, m_midi_note;
-	std::array<uint8_t, 64> m_fast_pad_queue;
-	uint8_t m_fast_pad_head, m_fast_pad_count;
+	// 512, not 64 - and the indices are 16-bit to match, because at 64 they
+	// were uint8_t and would wrap.
+	//
+	// 64 bytes cannot hold the boot handshake and the 26-byte power-on banner
+	// together, so the banner's tail was dropped on every single start: the
+	// counter read 128 drops, all of byte 0x39, which is the '9' of
+	// "Feb.15,1999". That is only the visible half. panel_hle_emit() drops a
+	// whole MESSAGE when the queue is full and does so SILENTLY, so a key press
+	// arriving during any burst simply never happened - which is why RESTART
+	// still misbehaved after the framing fix: the event was not corrupted, it
+	// was discarded.
+	//
+	// The queue drains at a byte every 4us, so 512 bytes is 2ms of backlog at
+	// worst and costs half a kilobyte.
+	std::array<uint8_t, 512> m_fast_pad_queue;
+	uint16_t m_fast_pad_head, m_fast_pad_count;
 };
 
 void mpc2000_state::machine_start()
@@ -856,7 +870,14 @@
 	// otherwise become a scheduler storm.
 	if ((m_fast_pad_count + 2) > m_fast_pad_queue.size())
 	{
+		// Say so. This dropped whole key presses in silence, which is
+		// indistinguishable from a controller that never sent them - and that
+		// is exactly where the search went first.
 		m_panel_hle_drops++;
+		if (!(m_panel_hle_drops % 16))
+			osd_printf_warning(
+					"MPC panel queue full; dropped message %02x %02x "
+					"(%u dropped)\n", status, data, m_panel_hle_drops);
 		return;
 	}
 	enqueue_fast_pad_byte(status);
